'''Knowledge management''' is the organized practice of creating, locating, sharing, retaining and applying knowledge in an organization or community. It includes people, processes and technology rather than merely storing documents.

== Knowledge forms and processes ==
Explicit artifacts include manuals, datasets, code and records; tacit knowledge includes skills and context that are difficult to articulate. Nonaka's 1994 organizational knowledge-creation model describes interactions between tacit and explicit knowledge. Its proposed modes of socialization, externalization, combination and internalization are an influential analytical framework, not exhaustive or automatic conversions. Information retrieval alone cannot guarantee comprehension or justified belief.

== Architecture and lifecycle ==
A maintained system needs defined ownership, identifiers, provenance, access controls, version history, search, review and retirement mechanisms. Separate assertions from discussions, evidence from conclusions and proposed updates from accepted records. For a recurring incident, a case record can preserve the observed symptoms, diagnostic evidence and applicable environment; a generalized procedure should be derived only after checking transfer conditions. Document review triggers and escalation for conflicting versions.

== Evaluation ==
Use a stratified set of real tasks to assess retrieval success, correctness of application, time to resolution, update latency and stale-answer rate. Compare against a baseline with independent outcome review. Participation counts and document volume are weak proxies for knowledge usefulness; incentives may generate redundant records. Privacy, intellectual property, unequal access and loss of tacit skills remain constraints. Knowledge management coordinates practices inside a knowledge ecosystem but is not synonymous with the ecosystem itself.

